I'm not quite as gloomy as you are about this because I think
our toy PPL's are poor models of real world PPL's , a crucial
element is missing, force. Suppose we get in a nasty dispute on
this list. We both think were right ( everybody always thinks
their right) and it's always fun to vent your spleen so we don't
compromise, no incentive to . What's the worse that can happen?
Well, the second worse  thing is that one of us gets kicked off
the list , not exactly a fate worse than death. The very worse
thing is that one of us is made to look stupid. If we change our
mind and back down too much many will also suspect we find
People Magazine too demanding. The solution is obvious, no
compromise ,attack , go for the jugular. Such behavior is common
on the net but only a psychopath would act that way in the real
world. If we both insist on escalation at every encounter it
will not stop till one or both of us is dead , a strong
incentive to act in a civilized manner.
	       
Real PPL's in a anarchic world would have private protection
agencies to back them up. All parties would have a reason to
avoid violence if possible. The disputing parties would not want
to turn their front yard into a war zone and violence is
expensive and the protection agencies are more interested in
making money than saving face. Most of the time this would work
so I expect the total level of violence to be less than what we
have now but I'm not such a utopian as to suggest it will drop
to zero. Even when force is not used the implicit threat is
always there, another good reason to be civilized.
	       
Our impotent PPL's don't have anything equivalent to private
protection agencies so I don't think we can learn much from them.
	       
