From: Marc Ringuette <mnr@cs.cmu.edu>
Date: Tue, 1 Feb 1994 02:13-EST
Subject: [#94-2-2] ECON: Wild Solar Power Idea

The solar cell discussion reminded me of a wild and potentially great
idea I heard from a friend of mine, that you might enjoy:

   Build a large fat cylinder with an open top - sort of like a
   nuclear power plant cooling tower, only 1/4 of a mile on a side.
   Locate it in a hot desert.  Rig sprinklers across the top of it,
   to spray a mist of water and cool the air.  The air falls.  Duct
   the air out the bottom of the cylinder, and when the wind has built
   to 50mph or so, use turbines to generate power.  Voila - gigawatts
   of power generated by your own miniature weather system.

Apparently, you have to build this thing big or not at all, in order to
get the wind moving and overcome the energy cost of pumping the water
to the top of the tower.  I haven't seen or worked through the numbers,
but it has one important quality of a Right Idea for solar power
generation:  the collector area is made up of a lightweight and cheap
material (namely, a light mist of water and a large body of air).


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu).  Freely forward/archive this.

