Arguments and dismissals I don't see as mutually exclusive, but my "by
and large" was intended to indicate that some folks didn't dismiss them,
but many did (myself included).  I can't summarise the arguments because
I only remember my conclusions/ dogmas /prejudices.  Summary:

I don't believe in nanarchy/blue-goo because:

-    I don't believe that self-replicating nano-assemblers will cause
     a phase change/singularity.  Nanotech will arrive slowly and
     gradually enough that society will adopt the new technology. 
     Defense strategies will evolve to immunise us from (IMO highly
     improbable) runaway grey-goo nightmares.

-    I have _never_ seen the problem of co-ordinating billions of self-
     replicating nano-assemblers on manufacturing macro-objects
     convincely addressed.  Usually the issue is just ignored.  Either
     self-replicating nano-assemblers are dumb or not self-directed and
     require Soviet style (mis-)management (and be at a competitive
     disadvantage) or they're intelligent and will evolve their own
     values.

-    I don't believe the solution it offers is workable/stable.  Blue-
     goo seems to rely on the notion that future growth and innovation
     can ever afterwards be predicted and controlled for the "greater
     good".  In other words blue-goo is Soviet style philosophy dressed
     up with nano-neologisms.

Hope that doesn't seem to close-minded, but everything about blue-goo
and nanarchy clashes with all my intuitions about anarchy, capitalism
and evolution.

