From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Date: Tue, 1 Feb 94 12:51:06 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-4] META: Another goodbye

I've reluctantly decided it's time for me to leave the list as well.  This
isn't because of any dissatisfaction with the content, but just that I
can't devote the time to it any more.  And the more interesting it gets,
the more time it takes.  The current thread concerning the arbitrariness of
our categorisations of experience is one of my favorite philosophical
topics.  And I'd be fascinated to follow the list's experiments with PPL --
this could be history in the making, if you get the present teething
troubles sorted out.  There's always been a lot to agree with here, a lot
to disagree with, and most of all, a lot to think about.  But I have to say
goodbye now.  I'll be sending my unsub in as soon as this message gets back
to me.

I don't want to engage in hit-and-run posting, but on the "Why can't we get
along" thread, there is one suggestion I'd like to make, based on my
experience of some contentious newsgroups such as talk.politics.guns and
soc.{bi,motss}.  The same anti-gun or anti-motss-sex arguments keep
cropping up there, and the stalwarts of those groups keep responding in a
civilised manner.  The attitude they take is that they are not writing to
convert the people their remarks are ostensibly addressed to.  Their real
audience is the lurkers.  They write to educate them, and to practise their
arguments for when they come up against opponents in the real world.  Of
course, unlike those newsgroups, this list isn't a place for debating the
basics over and over, but the same principle can still apply to the debates
we do have.  In short, beside the cocktail party metaphor that many have
used, I would suggest the metaphor of a panel discussion before a largely
silent audience.  And since that is shortly to become a paying audience,
they need to get their money's worth or they won't stay.  Just a thought.

I'll still be on the ExI-UK list, the new General Semantics list, the novis
list, and a few other more tenuously extropian places on the net, like
zendo, soc.bi, uk-motss, and alt.sex.bondage.  (Well, *I* find these things
are all connected. :-))  And email, of course, at least until the end of
March 1995 when my current contract ends.  I'm currently looking for job
possibilities for a theoretical computer scientist, preferably in or near a
big city in an English-speaking country.

