I said:

>> If we can't get PPLs to work on the List, amongst highly
>> intelligent committed extropians, then we can forget about it
>> _ever_ working in the real-world

John K Clark makes a very good point about coercion and private
protection agencies, or PPAs,:

> I'm not quite as gloomy as you are about this because I think
> our toy PPL's are poor models of real world PPL's , a crucial
> element is missing, force. [...]  Real PPL's in a anarchic world
> would have private protection agencies to back them up. [...]
> Our impotent PPL's don't have anything equivalent to private
> protection agencies so I don't think we can learn much from them.

I agree that the element of PPL/PPA supplied force is missing, but I
think it might still supply a good model of the world.

We live in a world dominated by coercive monopolistic suppliers of law
(states and their appointed regulatory bodies).  We seek to erode the
power of these coercive monopolies.  This isn't go to happen overnight
and the transition period will require "persuading" the CMs to gradually
relinquish control.  On the List ExI plays the role of the Evil State
:-) and our job, as committed List anarchists, is to find a transitional
path down which ExI can be persuaded to move.

The InterPPL charter is halfway between List Law being the sole
prerogative of Exi and the completely anarchic situation of List Law
supplied by PPAs, decoupled from ExI.  The InterPPL charter is an
attempt to create an toy environment which simulates some transitional
position.  List Law is supplied through PPLs and ExI creates, changes
and winds up the PPLs.  After ExI and us have gained confidence in the
use of List Law PPLs we can start introducing something analogous to
private protection agencies so PPLs (now PPAs) can nuke each other.

In war, I've heard, a 3-to-1 superiority is regarded prudent to achieve
victory (else some type inconclusive armistice results).  How about:
Anyone can create a PPA, but a PPA can be nuked by another PPA or PPA
collective of 3 or more times its membership base.  If a PPA is nuked
its members, unless in another PPA, become Outlaws.  Membership totals
would need publishing, perhaps once a week, as PPAs "size up" to each
other and form alliances.

Let's see if the PPL experiment works first.

